    "message": "feat(request-log): compact existing history, and deduplicate bodies\n\nCompression only ever applied to newly written requests, so a database\ncarried across the 4.45 upgrade kept paying the old price for its whole\nhistory: on a real 1.7 GB log, every one of its 50,000 rows was still\nstored inline and uncompressed. Reported as \"didn't you do some\ncompression, what happened to it\".\n\nfcc-compact-log rewrites them in place. Measured on a copy of that real\ndatabase: 1.73 GB -> 0.29 GB in 4.9 minutes, and all 49,934 bodies then\nverified byte-identical against the original. Safe to interrupt: batches\ncommit individually and a row is converted only after its body is\nstored, so a kill leaves a consistent database with the work unfinished.\nRunning it again resumes.\n\nBodies are now content-addressed by SHA-256 of their packed form, so\nidentical content is stored once and a repeat skips compression\nentirely. Blobs are collected only when the last request referencing\nthem is pruned.\n\nHonest note on the size of that win: inputs repeat 62.7% of the time,\nbut whole bodies almost never do, because two requests sharing a prompt\ndiffer in their reply. Whole-body dedup found 1.4% on a real log.\nSplitting the fields into separate blobs would capture the input\nduplication and is the obvious next step, not done here.\n\nThe compaction also widens page_size to 16 KB, since a 7 KB body always\noverflows a 4 KB page and the vacuum has to rewrite the file anyway.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
